# Revision history # {#revision-history}

## R3 (2026-10-21) ## {#revision-r3}

- Updated the author list to reflect the current active contributors, with James Touton credited as the original author.
- Retargeted the proposal to C++29 and rebased the wording on the current working draft.
- Replaced the separate pack-length component of specialization identity with an invented constant template parameter for the count.
- Added Abseil and CasADi use cases with comparisons to current C++ alternatives.
- Updated pack classification, deduction, initialization, address-taking, and lambda rules for the count model and C++26 pack indexing.
- Separated named homogeneous packs from optional reclamation of deprecated comma-less C varargs, with independent EWG direction polls.

## R2 (2019-10-05) ## {#revision-r2}

- Rebased wording against N4830.
- Added discussion on the impact of making varargs commas mandatory.

## R1 (2019-03-11) ## {#revision-r1}

- Rebased wording against N4800.
- Added wording for signature changes.
- Removed wording permitting empty template parameter lists for template declarations.
- Added feature test macro.
- Added discussion of alternative designs to address or avoid confusion with explicit specializations.
- Added straw polls from the Evolution Working Group Incubator (EWGI) in Kona.
- Added acknowledgments.
- Minor corrections to examples.

## R0 (2018-10-08) ## {#revision-r0}

- Initial draft. [[P1219R0]]

# Introduction # {#introduction}

This paper proposes homogeneous function parameter packs for C++29, allowing a single declaration to replace a family of fixed-arity overloads while retaining ordinary conversions. Such a pack would have one declared element type and use ordinary parameter initialization.

Note: Clause references use the current C++ working draft. [[CPP-DRAFT]]

<figure class=table id=pack-matrix>
<figcaption id=pack-matrix-caption>Independent parameter-pack declarations. C++26 permits all but the proposed lower-right form.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison" aria-labelledby=pack-matrix-caption>
<colgroup><col><col><col></colgroup>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Element types</th><th scope=col>Template parameter pack</th><th scope=col>Function parameter pack</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope=row>Heterogeneous</th>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <auto... vs>
void f();
</xmp>
</td>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
void f(auto... vs);
</xmp>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope=row>Homogeneous</th>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Legal since C++11: T is not a parameter pack, but vs is.
template <class T, T... vs>
void foo();
</xmp>
</td>
<td class=code-invalid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Proposed (ill-formed in C++26): vs is a pack of T parameters.
template <class T>
void foo(T... vs);
</xmp>
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

As Table 1 shows, the symmetry between template and function parameter packs is incomplete: C++26 lacks the homogeneous function form. In the lower-right declaration, <code>T</code> names one type, leaving <code>T... vs</code> with no pack to expand. The declaration is therefore ill-formed in current C++. <a href="[STD]temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a> <a href="[STD]dcl.spec.auto.general#2">[dcl.spec.auto.general]/2</a> <a href="[STD]dcl.fct#26">[dcl.fct]/26</a> Under our proposal, <code>vs</code> would instead be a pack of parameters, each of type <code>T</code>.

For fixed or explicitly supplied <code>T</code>, ordinary conversions can initialize parameters from different source types. Deduced <code>T</code> requires consistent deductions across applicable arguments. <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.type#2">[temp.deduct.type]/2</a> The pack's length is known at compile time even when its arguments are runtime values, unlike the constant template arguments of homogeneous template packs. <a href="[STD]temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a>

The proposal allows one trailing homogeneous pack, making an abbreviated function template whose specializations have ordinary fixed-arity function types. Existing pack expansions retain their meanings.

Earlier revisions received EWGI support, though the EWG poll to accept P1219 for C++23 failed in Belfast in 2019. [[P1219-EWG-RECORD]] Since then, C++26 has deprecated the comma-less C-varargs spelling that conflicts with unnamed homogeneous packs. <a href="[STD]depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a> We return with concrete library comparisons, further evidence of programmer demand, and a revised model for the pack count.

A single trailing pack covers the library use cases and lets the remaining arguments determine its length. We defer declaration-site lengths, which need new syntax, and multiple packs, which need argument-partitioning rules ([[#explicit-lengths]]).

Named packs such as <code>int... values</code> can be adopted independently of the <a href="#compatibility">decision to reclaim comma-less C varargs</a> for unnamed packs. We therefore propose <a href="#proposed-polls">separate EWG polls</a>. Deprecation alone does not establish safe reclamation. A separate removal paper remains a fallback if EWG feedback warrants it.

# Library use cases # {#examples}


## Combine call-site diagnostics with count-dependent results ## {#example-count}

Abseil's internal <code>Layout&lt;Ts...&gt;</code> describes one array per element type in a single allocation. <code>Partial</code> records how many leading array sizes are supplied in its result type <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code>, though the sizes may be runtime values. [[ABSL-LAYOUT]]

With runtime <code>size_t</code> values <code>int_count</code> and <code>double_count</code>:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
using ArrayLayout =
    absl::container_internal::Layout<int, double, char>;

auto ints_known = ArrayLayout::Partial(int_count);
auto ints_and_doubles_known =
    ArrayLayout::Partial(int_count, double_count);
</xmp>

These calls return <code>ArrayLayout::PartialType&lt;1&gt;</code> and <code>ArrayLayout::PartialType&lt;2&gt;</code>, respectively. The first permits querying <code>int</code> and <code>double</code> offsets, while the second also permits querying the <code>char</code> offset:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto double_offset = ints_known.Offset<double>();
auto char_offset = ints_and_doubles_known.Offset<char>();
</xmp>

<code>ints_known.Offset&lt;char&gt;()</code> is rejected at compile time because the preceding <code>double</code> size is missing. A replacement must preserve these count-selected result types and available offsets even when sizes are runtime values. [[ABSL-LAYOUT]]

To obtain that count, the shipped declaration deduces every argument type. In the body, it casts each argument to <code>size_t</code>. A homogeneous pack would instead deduce only the count and initialize <code>size_t</code> parameters at the call:

<figure class=table id=partial-comparison>
<figcaption id=partial-comparison-caption>Abseil's <code>Partial</code> and a proposed homogeneous declaration. Both retain the argument-count check.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison" aria-labelledby=partial-comparison-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Abseil at d522178</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Sizes>
static constexpr PartialType<sizeof...(Sizes)>
Partial(Sizes&&... sizes) {
  static_assert(sizeof...(Sizes) +
      StaticSizeSeq::size() <= sizeof...(Ts));
  return PartialType<sizeof...(Sizes)>(
      static_cast<size_t>(
          std::forward<Sizes>(sizes))...);
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
static constexpr auto
Partial(size_t... sizes)
    -> PartialType<sizeof...(sizes)> {
  static_assert(sizeof...(sizes) +
      StaticSizeSeq::size() <= sizeof...(Ts));
  return PartialType<sizeof...(sizes)>(
      sizes...);
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

For <code>int signed_count</code>, Abseil's authors want warnings for <code>ArrayLayout::Partial(signed_count)</code> but not <code>ArrayLayout::Partial(3)</code>. Their <a href="https://github.com/abseil/abseil-cpp/blob/d522178b54087d2c3855484c1899ce99ad278169/absl/container/internal/layout.h#L760-L780">comment</a> explains that explicit casts suppress both warnings, while implicit forwarding-body conversions can warn for both.

Fixed <code>size_t</code> parameters make this distinction available by exposing the original argument expression to conversion diagnostics at the call. A <code>std::convertible_to&lt;size_t&gt;</code> constraint, however, leaves conversions in the body. <a href="[STD]concept.convertible#1">[concept.convertible]/1</a> <a href="[STD]expr.call#7">[expr.call]/7</a>

Whether these conversions produce warnings depends on the compiler and enabled warning options. Negative integers remain well-formed and convert to unsigned values. Also, ordinary parameter initialization rejects explicit-only conversions, such as an explicit <code>operator size_t()</code>, that the shipped <code>static_cast</code> accepts. <a href="[STD]conv.integral#3">[conv.integral]/3</a> <a href="[STD]over.match.conv#1">[over.match.conv]/1</a> <a href="[STD]expr.static.cast#5">[expr.static.cast]/5</a>

For plain <code>Layout&lt;Ts...&gt;</code> without static sizes, current C++ can preserve the conversions and result types with overloads for arities zero through <code>sizeof...(Ts)</code>. The header's <a href="https://github.com/abseil/abseil-cpp/blob/d522178b54087d2c3855484c1899ce99ad278169/absl/container/internal/layout.h#L399-L400">constructor</a> already expands an index pack through <code>IntToSize</code> to obtain <code>size_t</code> parameters. Here <code>LayoutType&lt;N, Ts...&gt;</code> stands for <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code>:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <size_t>
using IntToSize = size_t;

template <class Seq, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverload;

template <size_t... Is, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverload<std::index_sequence<Is...>, Ts...> {
  static constexpr LayoutType<sizeof...(Is), Ts...>
  Partial(IntToSize<Is>... sizes) {
    return LayoutType<sizeof...(Is), Ts...>(sizes...);
  }
};

template <class Seq, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverloads;

template <size_t... Ns, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverloads<std::index_sequence<Ns...>, Ts...>
    : PartialOverload<std::make_index_sequence<Ns>, Ts...>... {
  using PartialOverload<std::make_index_sequence<Ns>, Ts...>::Partial...;
};
</xmp>

To expose the overload set, <code>Layout</code> inherits <code>PartialOverloads&lt;std::make_index_sequence&lt;sizeof...(Ts) + 1&gt;, Ts...&gt;</code>.

A homogeneous pack replaces this generation and combination with one declaration.

## Preserve conversion ranking without fixed-arity wrappers ## {#example-casadi}

CasADi builds symbolic matrix expressions, with <code>horzcat</code>, <code>vertcat</code>, and <code>diagcat</code> providing horizontal, vertical, and diagonal concatenation. At <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/commit/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11">d30fbc4</a>, these operations have fifteen positional wrappers covering arities two through six. Their <code>const MatType&amp;</code> parameter types are fixed by the enclosing <code>SparsityInterface</code> specialization. [[CASADI-CONCAT]] For example, the library call <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11/casadi/core/integration_tools.cpp#L930-L936"><code>vertcat(dx_cat, 1)</code></a> relies on scalar conversion to that matrix type.

<figure class=table id=casadi-comparison>
<figcaption id=casadi-comparison-caption>CasADi's vertical concatenation wrappers, with two arities shown, and a proposed replacement for the family. Both initialize references to a fixed matrix type.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison" aria-labelledby=casadi-comparison-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>CasADi at <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/commit/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11">d30fbc4</a></th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
inline friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& x, const MatType& y) {
  return vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{x, y});
}

inline friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& x, const MatType& y,
    const MatType& z) {
  return vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{x, y, z});
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& first,
    const MatType& second,
    const MatType&... rest) {
  return MatType::vertcat(
      std::vector<MatType>{first, second, rest...});
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

In the proposed declaration, the fixed parameters retain the two-operand minimum, while the trailing pack removes the six-argument ceiling. The same change would apply to horizontal and diagonal concatenation.

A constrained deduced pack in the same class also replaces the overload family:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Args>
  requires (sizeof...(Args) >= 2 &&
            (std::is_convertible_v<
                const Args&, const MatType&> && ...))
friend MatType vertcat(const Args&... args) {
  return MatType::vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{args...});
}
</xmp>

CasADi's <code>SX</code> and <code>IM</code> are symbolic-expression and integer matrices. For mutable <code>SX symbolic</code> and <code>IM indices</code>, <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11/casadi/core/generic_matrix.hpp#L285-L288">indexing</a> yields a <code>SubIndex&lt;SX, IM&gt;</code> proxy derived from <code>SX</code>. [[CASADI-PROXY]] Now consider these constructed calls and how overload resolution changes:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
vertcat(symbolic(indices), 1);
vertcat(1, symbolic(indices));
</xmp>

Argument-dependent lookup finds concatenation functions for <code>SX</code> and the proxy's <code>IM</code> template argument. <a href="[STD]basic.lookup.argdep#3">[basic.lookup.argdep]/3</a> Fixed parameters select <code>SX</code> in both calls because binding the proxy to its base outranks conversion to <code>IM</code>. <a href="[STD]over.ics.rank#2.1">[over.ics.rank]/2.1</a> If we consider a deduced pack instead, both candidates bind the deduced types directly and satisfy the constraints. Neither wins. Therefore, both calls are ambiguous. <a href="[STD]over.match.best.general#2">[over.match.best.general]/2</a> The homogeneous declaration retains the fixed destinations for conversion ranking.

Fixing only the first parameter as <code>const MatType&amp;</code> still leaves the second call ambiguous. Even two fixed parameters leave <code>vertcat(1, 1, symbolic(indices))</code> ambiguous because the distinguishing operand is in the deduced tail.

Grouped calls to competing <code>std::initializer_list&lt;SX&gt;</code> and <code>std::initializer_list&lt;IM&gt;</code> overloads are also ambiguous. List ranking uses the worst element conversion, so the scalar's user-defined conversion to either destination erases the indexed operand's distinction. <a href="[STD]over.ics.list#5">[over.ics.list]/5</a>

Current C++ does permit arbitrary counts when the destination is explicit, such as in <code>SX::vertcat({symbolic(indices), 1})</code>. Existing or generated fixed-arity wrappers also preserve positional calls, but a homogeneous pack would express the intended interface directly: two or more parameters of type <code>const MatType&amp;</code>. One declaration per operation would preserve the wrappers' conversion ranking while removing their fixed upper bound and the need to maintain separate overloads.

# Motivation and existing alternatives # {#motivation}


## Demand for fixed-type variadic interfaces ## {#expressivity}

<p>The examples in R2 and later requests for fixed-type positional interfaces show that this requirement recurs. [[P1219R2]] [[REDDIT-ENUM-PACK]] [[SO-STRING-VIEW-CONCAT]]</p>

<p>These discussions concern conversions left in function bodies [[SO-TYPED-ACCUMULATOR]], ordinary conversion ranking [[LOEW-HOMOGENEOUS-OVERLOADS]], and by-value parameter initialization without additional construction in the body. [[MAZIERES-PARAM-PACK]] A homogeneous pack's count would also permit constraints, such as requiring an even number of arguments. [[SO-10]]</p>

<p>These discussions provide solutions in current C++, including constrained packs and emulations with fixed parameter types. We consider them inferior for these interfaces because they make a simple parameter-type requirement depend on constraints, helper templates, or generated overloads. Our proposal would state the element type directly in the parameter list, making the code substantially easier to write and reason about while preserving ordinary initialization and conversion ranking.</p>

## Initializer lists and vectors ## {#initializer-lists}

Initializer lists and vectors also accept elements of a fixed type, but they expose a sequence rather than separate function parameters. <a href="[STD]support.initlist">[support.initlist]</a> <a href="[STD]vector.overview">[vector.overview]</a>

<figure class=table id=homogeneous-interfaces>
<figcaption id=homogeneous-interfaces-caption>Homogeneous interfaces, each permitting a fixed element type. The vector column excludes <code>bool</code>.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=homogeneous-interfaces-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Property</th><th scope=col>Homogeneous pack</th><th scope=col><code>std::initializer_list&lt;T&gt;</code></th><th scope=col><code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;</code></th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope=row>Elements and mutation</th><td>Value or reference parameters with declared cv-qualification</td><td>Const backing-array elements</td><td>Owned elements, mutable through a non-const vector</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Ownership and lifetime</th><td>Values owned, references borrowed under ordinary lifetime rules</td><td>View. Copies neither copy elements nor extend backing-array lifetime</td><td>Owns storage and elements. Reference parameters borrow</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Contiguous storage</th><td>No array or contiguity guarantee</td><td>Contiguous backing array</td><td>Contiguous storage</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Count and iteration</th><td>Template count. Pack expansion, folds, or pack indexing</td><td><code>size()</code> and ordinary iteration</td><td><code>size()</code> and ordinary iteration</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Runtime-sized input</th><td>No direct runtime-range expansion into arguments</td><td>No arbitrary runtime-range constructor</td><td>Accepts runtime-sized input and can grow</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Storage and element work</th><td>Ordinary parameter initialization, no container allocation required</td><td>Backing-array initialization</td><td>Storage construction can allocate and construct elements. Passing an existing vector by reference does neither</td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Call syntax</th><td><code>f(a, b, c)</code></td><td><code>f({a, b, c})</code></td><td><code>f(existing)</code> or vector construction, such as <code>f({a, b, c})</code></td></tr>
<tr><th scope=row>Function declarations</th><td>Template with one trailing homogeneous pack</td><td>Can be non-template and precede other parameters</td><td>Can be non-template and precede other parameters</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

## Spans, arrays, and constrained ranges ## {#range-alternatives}

Static spans, arrays, and array references can already supply the compile-time count needed by Abseil's <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code>. The count comes from the input type, so the elements can still be runtime values. Our proposal would obtain the same count from separate positional arguments while retaining ordinary parameter initialization. For comparison, these existing-C++ functions deduce the count from a static span or array reference: <a href="[STD]span.overview">[span.overview]</a> <a href="[STD]array#overview">[array.overview]</a> <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t N>
auto span_count(std::span<const std::size_t, N>) {
    return std::integral_constant<std::size_t, N>{};
}

template <std::size_t N>
auto array_count(const std::size_t (&)[N]) {
    return std::integral_constant<std::size_t, N>{};
}

const std::size_t sizes[]{3, 5};
auto from_span = span_count(std::span{sizes});
auto from_array = array_count(sizes);
auto from_braces = array_count({3, 5});
</xmp>

All three results are <code>std::integral_constant&lt;std::size_t, 2&gt;</code>. In particular, <code>array_count({3, 5})</code> deduces the bound and initializes a temporary array. An analogous array-reference <code>Partial({int_count, double_count})</code> could therefore return <code>PartialType&lt;2&gt;</code> without named storage or an explicit extent. This meets the count requirement in current C++. <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a>

The difference is argument initialization. The braced array uses list-initialization, which rejects the narrowing conversions that ordinary <code>size_t</code> parameters permit in Abseil's signed-variable case ([[#example-count]]). The array-reference form also cannot accept an empty list, because built-in arrays require positive bounds. Static spans and <code>std::array</code> can represent zero extent, but still require the arguments to be grouped. <a href="[STD]dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> <a href="[STD]dcl.array#1">[dcl.array]/1</a> <a href="[STD]span.overview">[span.overview]</a> <a href="[STD]array.zero">[array.zero]</a> A homogeneous <code>size_t</code> pack would permit that signed-variable conversion and a zero-argument call while retaining positional syntax.

Passing an existing array through a span avoids constructing a new array at the call, but the element types must match apart from permitted qualification conversions. An <code>int</code> array therefore cannot initialize <code>std::span&lt;const std::size_t&gt;</code>, even though ordinary <code>size_t</code> parameters could accept its elements individually. <a href="[STD]span.cons#13">[span.cons]/13</a> <a href="[STD]span.cons#16">[span.cons]/16</a>

To convert the elements, callers can instead construct an explicitly typed <code>std::array&lt;T, N&gt;</code> or vector from a braced list. A const-element span can also accept a temporary array through double braces such as <code>&#123;&#123;1, 2}}</code>. Both approaches allow element conversions, but retain the list-initialization restrictions described above. The temporary array lasts through the call's full-expression. <a href="[STD]array#overview">[array.overview]</a> <a href="[STD]span.cons#13">[span.cons]/13</a> <a href="[STD]dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> <a href="[STD]class.temporary#6.10">[class.temporary]/6.10</a> Under our proposal, callers could pass the values directly, with each initializing its own parameter of the declared type.

Choosing a container's element type also settles the conversion destination before the call. CasADi's positional interface instead lets overload resolution choose that destination by ranking each argument's conversion ([[#example-casadi]]). Homogeneous packs would preserve this selection without requiring callers to choose the matrix type or group their arguments.

A constrained <code>std::ranges::input_range</code> is useful when the intended input is a sequence, particularly one that is runtime-sized or non-contiguous. The concept itself supplies no compile-time count, though deduction can retain one carried by an array or static-span type. Constraining element convertibility does not itself perform conversions or provide a separate parameter for each original argument. It therefore does not reproduce Abseil's call-site conversion diagnostics ([[#example-count]]). <a href="[STD]range.refinements">[range.refinements]</a> <a href="[STD]concept.convertible">[concept.convertible]</a> Homogeneous packs would expose each original argument to conversion diagnostics at the call while deducing a compile-time count from the argument list.

# Design decisions # {#design-decisions}

## Definitions ## {#definitions}

For each <em>homogeneous function parameter pack</em> (<em>homogeneous pack</em>), the proposal invents a constant template parameter for its length.

We classify function parameter packs by their <em>parameter pattern</em>, which is the declaration without the declarator ellipsis. <a href="[STD]temp.variadic#6.1">[temp.variadic]/6.1</a> Ordinary placeholder processing first invents a type parameter pack for <code>auto...</code>. <a href="[STD]dcl.fct#23">[dcl.fct]/23</a> We then classify the declaration before substituting enclosing template arguments: it is homogeneous only if its pattern contains no unexpanded pack. An ordinary expansion therefore remains an expansion even when its resulting parameter types are identical.

For this classification, an occurrence is unexpanded unless it is inside a nested expansion's pattern. Indexing consumes the indexed pack name, but leaves unexpanded packs in the index for an enclosing expansion. Neither <code>decltype</code> nor ordinary <code>sizeof</code> consumes packs. These cases follow the existing expansion boundaries <a href="[STD]temp.variadic#6">[temp.variadic]/6-8</a> and constraint rules <a href="[STD]temp.constr.fold">[temp.constr.fold]</a>.

In the following table, <code>xs</code> can denote a constant template parameter pack, function parameter pack, init-capture pack, or structured binding pack.

<figure class=table id=pack-classification>
<figcaption id=pack-classification-caption>Parameter-pack classification under this proposal.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=pack-classification-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Parameter declaration</th><th scope=col>Unexpanded packs</th><th scope=col>Meaning under the proposal</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>int... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous <code>int</code> pack</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>T... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous <code>T</code> pack</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto... values</code></td><td>Invented type pack</td><td>Ordinary expansion</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Ts... values</code></td><td><code>Ts</code></td><td>Expands <code>Ts</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Templates&lt;T&gt;... values</code></td><td><code>Templates</code></td><td>Expands <code>Templates</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(xs)... values</code></td><td><code>xs</code></td><td>Expands <code>xs</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(sizeof(xs))... values</code></td><td><code>xs</code></td><td>Expands <code>xs</code> despite identical resulting types</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Box&lt;Ts...&gt;... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous resulting <code>Box</code> type. Inner expansion consumes <code>Ts</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Ts...&#91;I]... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack of the selected type</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Ts...[Is]... values</code></td><td><code>Is</code></td><td>Expands <code>Is</code>. Indexing consumes <code>Ts</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(xs...&#91;I])... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack using the selected element's <code>decltype</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(xs...[Is])... values</code></td><td><code>Is</code></td><td>Expands <code>Is</code>. Indexing consumes <code>xs</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(xs...&#91;I] + xs)... values</code></td><td>The second <code>xs</code></td><td>Expands the second <code>xs</code>. Indexing consumes only the first</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype((xs + ...))... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack of the fold's result type</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(sizeof...(xs))... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous pack of the <code>sizeof...</code> result type</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int... values[Is]</code></td><td><code>Is</code></td><td>Expands <code>Is</code> despite array parameter adjustment to <code>int*</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>decltype(requires { requires (C&lt;Ts&gt; &amp;&amp; ...); })... values</code></td><td>Nothing</td><td>Homogeneous <code>bool</code> pack. Nested fold-expanded constraint consumes <code>Ts</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

Each homogeneous case gets its own count, but a zero count does not avoid substitution into the element type. Types, indices, folds, and array bounds must still be valid. Also, the last row's nested requirement determines the value of a <code>bool</code> requires-expression without constraining the function.

## Pack declarations ## {#implicit-template}

The same declarator syntax covers ordinary expansions and homogeneous packs, whose patterns need not mention template parameters:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// heterogeneous function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T... v);

// homogeneous function parameter pack
template <class T>
void f(T... v);

// homogeneous function parameter pack
void f(int... v);
</xmp>

R2 treated the pack length as an additional part of specialization identity even when the template had no formal parameters. [[P1219R2]] In this revision, a homogeneous function pack makes the declaration an abbreviated function template before body parsing, with an invented, unnamed constant template parameter representing its length. The length is therefore a template argument, whose value can be deduced or supplied explicitly where its position allows. For named packs, <code>sizeof...(values)</code> also exposes it.

The count has type <code>std::size_t</code>, matching the type of a <code>sizeof...</code> expression. To avoid requiring either a <code>std::size_t</code> declaration or a header, the wording refers to the type of a <code>sizeof</code> expression. <a href="[STD]expr.sizeof#5">[expr.sizeof]/5</a>

There is a related concern in [[P1061R10]] section 3.3, which removed support for structured binding packs outside templates after objections to R9's implicit template region. That region covered the rest of an ordinary function after a structured binding declaration, whereas here the signature declares the whole function a template. This distinction does not, however, establish committee agreement on the proposal.

A homogeneous pack must be the only function parameter pack, appear last, and have no following C ellipsis. Non-pack parameters may precede it, as in [[#example-casadi]], leaving the remaining arguments to determine its count without pack or C-ellipsis partitioning rules. Once formed, its parameters behave like those of any function parameter pack.

These declarations violate the placement rules:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void misplaced(int... values, int last);
void two_packs(int... left, double... right);
void trailing_varargs(int... values, ...);
</xmp>

The pack must also declare the function's or lambda call operator's own parameters. For example, <code>using Callback = void(int... values);</code> and <code>void register_callback(void (*callback)(int... values));</code> remain ill-formed because neither declares a function template to own the count. Supporting these forms would need separate count-introduction and deduction rules, while ordinary expansions in aliases and nested function types remain unchanged.

Although [[P2347R2]] proposes deducing the length of a single non-trailing function parameter pack from the number of arguments and required trailing parameters, non-trailing homogeneous packs remain outside this revision's scope.

### Abbreviated syntax ### {#abbreviated-syntax}

Since C++20, function templates needing no template parameter names can use abbreviated syntax with placeholders such as <code>auto</code> in the parameter list. [[P1141R2]] <a href="[STD]dcl.fct#23">[dcl.fct]/23</a>

Under our proposal, a fixed element type likewise leaves a template parameter for the count. When there are no explicit template parameters, the declaration omits the <code nohighlight>template-head</code>. An empty <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> still introduces an explicit specialization and cannot introduce a homogeneous pack.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void named_f(int... values);
auto* named_pf = &named_f<3>;

template <class T>
void named_g(T... values);
auto* named_pg = &named_g<int, 3>;
</xmp>

Both pointers have type <code>void (*)(int, int, int)</code>. Reclamation would additionally permit omitting the parameter names.

The count follows all explicit template parameters and invented <code>auto</code> type parameters without changing their positions:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class Tag>
void tagged(auto tag, int... values);
auto* tagged_pair = &tagged<void, char, 2>;
</xmp>

Here the arguments supply <code>Tag</code>, the invented <code>tag</code> type, and then the count, yielding pointer type <code>void (*)(char, int, int)</code>. The same order applies to lambda call operator templates.

Because the homogeneous pack must be last, this also follows the order of the corresponding function parameters. Supporting non-trailing homogeneous packs is outside the scope of this paper and would require revisiting the ordering rule ([[#explicit-lengths]]).


<aside class=issue>
<p>Explicit-argument assignment remains to be specified when template parameter packs precede the count, including constant packs and <code>auto</code> parameters. The count remains last.</p>
</aside>

### Type deduction ### {#deduction}

To see how type deduction interacts with the count, consider these proposed functions. <code>sum</code> fixes the element type to <code>int</code>, whereas <code>total</code> uses the ordinary template parameter <code>T</code>.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
int sum(int... values) {
    return (0 + ... + values);
}

template <class T>
T total(T... values) {
    return (T{} + ... + values);
}
</xmp>

For <code>total</code>, deductions of <code>T</code> from all applicable arguments must agree. It neither takes the first argument's type nor computes a common type. <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a> <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.type#2">[temp.deduct.type]/2</a> Once the element type is fixed, as in <code>sum</code> or <code>total&lt;int&gt;</code>, ordinary conversions initialize the <code>int</code> parameters. <a href="[STD]expr.call#7">[expr.call]/7</a> Supplying <code>T</code> alone leaves count deduction unchanged. Explicit counts fix the length, as in <code>sum&lt;2&gt;(1, 2.5)</code> or <code>total&lt;int, 2&gt;(1, 2.5)</code>.

<figure class=table id=deduced-calls>
<figcaption id=deduced-calls-caption>Proposed calls. Counts are omitted and deduced from arity.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=deduced-calls-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Element type</th><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td rowspan=2>Fixed <code>int</code></td><td><code>sum()</code></td><td>Length zero. Initialized addition fold returns zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sum(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Length two. Ordinary conversions initialize both parameters.</td></tr>
  <tr><td rowspan=3>Deduced <code>T</code></td><td><code>total(1, 2)</code></td><td>Deduces <code>T</code> as <code>int</code>, length two.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Reject conflicting deductions for <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total()</code></td><td>Reject: no argument to deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td rowspan=2>Explicit <code>int</code></td><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Length two, explicit type and ordinary conversions.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;()</code></td><td>Length zero, known element type.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

The template and its arguments, including the count converted to the count parameter's type, identify each specialization. The homogeneous pack's original argument types do not affect that identity when its element type is fixed. Therefore, <code>sum(1, 2)</code>, <code>sum(1.0, 2.0)</code>, and <code>sum&lt;2&gt;(1, 2)</code> all select <code>sum&lt;2&gt;</code>, whether the count is explicit or deduced.

Equal function types alone do not, however, identify specializations, because different template arguments may produce the same adjusted function type. <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.general#3">[temp.deduct.general]/3-4</a>

An explicit count must match the number of remaining call arguments. Thus <code>sum&lt;3&gt;(1, 2, 3)</code> and <code>sum&lt;0&gt;()</code> are valid, while <code>sum&lt;3&gt;(1, 2)</code>, <code>sum&lt;2&gt;(1, 2, 3)</code>, and <code>sum&lt;0&gt;(1)</code> are rejected. Counts follow ordinary constant template argument rules for the invented unsigned type. With no explicit count, <code>sum&lt;&gt;()</code> deduces zero. The invented parameter does not make every body expression dependent.

With zero count, the element type must be explicit, deduced elsewhere, or defaulted. Substitution into its pattern still applies.

Ordinary reference binding and parameter adjustment apply. <a href="[STD]dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> Braced arguments retain list-initialization and narrowing checks. <a href="[STD]dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> A temporary bound to a reference parameter lasts through the call's full-expression. <a href="[STD]class.temporary#6.10">[class.temporary]/6.10</a> Packs neither extend this lifetime nor change argument-initialization evaluation order. <a href="[STD]expr.call#7">[expr.call]/7-8</a>

### Target types for initialization ### {#parameter-initialization}

A fixed parameter type also provides a target when an argument is a braced initializer or an overloaded function name. Consider these proposed declarations:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

void plot(const Point&... points);

using Step = void (*)();
void start();
template <class T> void start(T mode);
void stop();

void run_all(Step... steps);
</xmp>

<figure class=table id=target-typing>
<figcaption id=target-typing-caption>Target typing under the proposed parameter-initialization rules.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=target-typing-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>plot({0, 0}, {4, 5})</code></td><td>Each initializes a temporary <code>Point</code> bound to a const reference parameter.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>plot({2.5, 3})</code></td><td>Reject: list-initialization narrows <code>Point::x</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>run_all(start, stop)</code></td><td>The <code>Step</code> target selects <code>void start()</code>.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

With <code>auto...</code> parameters instead, deduction cannot infer types from bare braces or the <code>start</code> overload set containing a function template. <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.type#5.8">[temp.deduct.type]/5.8-5.9</a> Current C++ can still supply targets through <code>std::initializer_list&lt;Point&gt;</code> and <code>std::initializer_list&lt;Step&gt;</code> parameters, called as <code>plot({&#123;0, 0}, {4, 5}})</code> and <code>run_all({start, stop})</code>. <a href="[STD]dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> Alternatively, callers can use explicit <code>Point</code> objects and <code>static_cast&lt;Step&gt;(start)</code> with deduced packs. <a href="[STD]over.over#1">[over.over]/1-3</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Deduction-guide support remains undecided pending analysis of deduction and return types.</p>
</aside>

### Address-taking ### {#addresses}

The count can also be deduced when taking the address of <code>sum</code> or <code>total</code>. Here the target function type supplies the count, with a length-two <code>sum</code> specialization having the ordinary type <code>int(int, int)</code>.

<figure class=table id=address-taking-cases>
<figcaption id=address-taking-cases-caption>Proposed address-taking and declarations. Rows are independent. Element type and count must match the identified function.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=address-taking-cases-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Operation</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>int (*zero)() = sum</code></td><td>Select length zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*one)(int) = sum&lt;&gt;</code><br><code>int (*two)(int, int) = sum&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>Target types select different specializations.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;sum</code><br><code>auto two = &amp;sum&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>Reject: no length determined.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;total&lt;int&gt;</code></td><td>Reject: explicit <code>T</code> supplies no length.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;sum&lt;2&gt;</code><br><code>auto two = &amp;total&lt;int, 2&gt;</code></td><td>Select two-parameter specialization without a target type.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*two)(int, int) = sum&lt;3&gt;</code></td><td>Reject: explicit length mismatches target.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*two)(double, double) = sum</code></td><td>Reject: parameter-type mismatch. Call conversions cannot adapt function pointer signatures.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int...)</code> and the named definition</td><td>Same template, conditional on comma-less unnamed reclamation.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int, int)</code></td><td>Ordinary overload, not an implicit specialization declaration.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

The proposal also retains existing explicit-specialization and explicit-instantiation syntax. <a href="[STD]temp.expl.spec#1">[temp.expl.spec]/1</a> <a href="[STD]temp.explicit#2">[temp.explicit]/2</a> For these declarations, omitted counts are deduced from the ordinary parameter lists:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <>
int sum<>(int value) {
    return value + 10;
}

template int sum<>(int, int);
</xmp>

Supplying the counts explicitly as <code>template &lt;&gt; int sum&lt;1&gt;(int value)</code> and <code>template int sum&lt;2&gt;(int, int)</code> identifies the same specialization and instantiation as above. The count must still match the parameter list.

### Overload ordering ### {#overload-ordering}

The proposal retains ordinary conversion ranking, partial ordering, constraint subsumption, and element-type comparisons. Under these rules, unordered candidates remain ambiguous, without preference for homogeneous syntax. Partial ordering, in particular, compares declarations, preserving which parameters came from packs. <a href="[STD]over.match.best.general#2">[over.match.best.general]/2</a> <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.partial#8">[temp.deduct.partial]/8</a> It therefore does not compare only resulting fixed-arity specializations. <a href="[STD]temp.func.order#3">[temp.func.order]/3-6</a>

Calls without pack arguments contribute no element-type comparisons to partial ordering. Default template arguments can establish viability without making dependent patterns fixed types for ordering.

<figure class=table id=overload-ordering-cases>
<figcaption id=overload-ordering-cases-caption>Proposed overloads under ordinary ordering. Sets are independent.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=overload-ordering-cases-caption>
<colgroup><col><col><col></colgroup>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Overloads</th><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td rowspan=3><code>void fixed(int... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void fixed(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>fixed(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the fixed-element template.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fixed(1.0, 2.0)</code></td><td>Select the deduced-element template by better conversions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fixed()</code></td><td>Select fixed-element template: <code>T</code> cannot be deduced.</td></tr>
<tr><td rowspan=3><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void packed(T... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class... Ts&gt; void packed(Ts... values);</code></td>
<td><code>packed(1, 2)</code></td><td>Ambiguous.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>packed(1, 2.0)</code></td><td>Select heterogeneous template: homogeneous <code>T</code> deductions conflict.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>packed()</code></td><td>Select heterogeneous template: homogeneous <code>T</code> cannot be deduced.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void empty(int... values);</code><br>
<code>void empty(double... values);</code></td>
<td><code>empty()</code></td><td>Ambiguous: zero count supplies no element-type comparison.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void pair(int, int);</code><br>
<code>void pair(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>pair(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the non-template overload after tied conversions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T, T);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>paired(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select fixed-arity template by ordinary partial ordering.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void single(T);</code><br>
<code>void single(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>single(1)</code></td><td>Ambiguous: neither the fixed-arity pattern nor fixed element type makes one template more specialized.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

For length-dependent associated constraints, count determination comes first. Satisfaction then substitutes ordinary template arguments, including the count and enclosing specializations' arguments, without separate count bindings. If the same atomic constraint is checked again with the same arguments, the existing consistency requirement still applies. <a href="[STD]temp.constr.atomic#3">[temp.constr.atomic]/3</a>

The count adds no atomic-identity or subsumption test. For example, separate predicates <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 1</code> and <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 2</code> gain no arithmetic implication rule. <a href="[STD]temp.constr.atomic#2">[temp.constr.atomic]/2</a> <a href="[STD]temp.constr.order#1.2">[temp.constr.order]/1.2</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p>The rules for matching corresponding packs in atomic and fold-expanded constraints remain incomplete.</p>
</aside>

### Constructors ### {#constructors}

If supported, homogeneous constructors would be implicit templates in ordinary overload selection. <a href="[STD]over.match.best.general#2.4">[over.match.best.general]/2.4</a> Initializer-list constructors, however, have priority under <a href="[STD]over.match.list#1">[over.match.list]/1</a>.

<figure class=table id=constructor-comparison>
<figcaption id=constructor-comparison-caption>Conditional comparison of initializer-list and homogeneous constructors.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class="data example-comparison" aria-labelledby=constructor-comparison-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>If homogeneous constructors are supported</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Values {
    Values(std::initializer_list<int>);
    explicit Values(const int&, const int&);
};
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Values {
    Values(const int&... elems);
    explicit Values(const int&, const int&);
};
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

<figure class=table id=constructor-selection>
<figcaption id=constructor-selection-caption>Constructor selection under ordinary ranking.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=constructor-selection-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Declaration</th><th scope=col>With initializer list</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous pack</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>Values values{1, 2, 3};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Selects the homogeneous constructor.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Values values{1, 2};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Selects the explicit two-argument constructor.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Values values = {1, 2};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Rejected: selected two-argument constructor is explicit.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Constructor support remains undecided. The examples above still need validation with homogeneous constructors treated as implicit templates.</p>
</aside>

## Lambdas ## {#lambdas}

The same count model also applies to lambdas. This proposed lambda's call operator remains a template despite its fixed element type:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto product = [](double... factors) {
    return (1.0 * ... * factors);
};
</xmp>

Here, <code>product()</code> and <code>product(0.5, 0.8)</code> return <code>1.0</code> and <code>0.4</code>. The invented count also lets <code>product.operator()&lt;2&gt;(0.5, 0.8)</code> select the second specialization. A current-language <code>std::same_as&lt;double&gt; auto... factors</code> lambda accepts both ordinary calls, though its template arguments are element types rather than a count.

For <code>double (*multiply)(double, double) = product</code>, the captureless lambda's generic conversion function template deduces count two from the target. Unary <code>+product</code>, however, lacks a target type and count and is rejected. Ordinary restrictions on capture, explicit object parameters, constraints, and static call operators also remain. <a href="[STD]expr.prim.lambda.closure#9">[expr.prim.lambda.closure]/9</a> <a href="[STD]expr.prim.lambda.closure#12">[expr.prim.lambda.closure]/12-14</a>

An enclosing pack's ordinary expansion, including indexed forms, needs no independent count and alone does not make a lambda generic. <a href="[STD]expr.prim.lambda.general#6">[expr.prim.lambda.general]/6</a>

<p id=captures>Homogeneous pack elements would follow ordinary capture and lifetime rules for function parameters. <a href="[STD]expr.prim.lambda.capture">[expr.prim.lambda.capture]</a></p>

## Pack indexing ## {#indexing}

Homogeneous packs would use existing expansion, folding, and constant-index access operations. <a href="[STD]temp.variadic">[temp.variadic]</a> <a href="[STD]expr.prim.pack.index#2">[expr.prim.pack.index]/2-4</a>

For example:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I>
double select_sample(double... samples)
    requires (I < sizeof...(samples)) {
    return samples...[I];
}
</xmp>

With runtime <code>double reading</code>, <code>select_sample&lt;1&gt;(reading, 0.5, 1.0)</code> selects the second sample using a supplied index and deduced length. Index 3 fails the constraint. A <code>std::same_as&lt;double&gt; auto... samples</code> pack gives the same results. <a href="[STD]expr.prim.pack.index#2">[expr.prim.pack.index]/2-4</a>

<code>Ts...[1]</code> selects the second type of <code>Ts</code>. As described in [[#definitions]], <code>Ts...[Is]</code> consumes <code>Ts</code> and leaves <code>Is</code> for an enclosing expansion. <a href="[STD]temp.variadic#6.15">[temp.variadic]/6.15</a> <a href="[STD]temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a> <a href="[STD]dcl.type.pack.index#2">[dcl.type.pack.index]/2-4</a>

The index pack can differ in length from <code>Ts</code> and repeat a type.

Rows in Table 12 are independent, with enclosing declarations of <code>Ts</code> and <code>Is</code> and valid selected indices.

<figure class=table id=indexed-lambda-parameters>
<figcaption id=indexed-lambda-parameters-caption>Indexed lambda parameters. A scalar index permits a new homogeneous count. An index pack produces an ordinary expansion.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=indexed-lambda-parameters-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Spelling</th><th scope=col>C++26 interpretation</th><th scope=col>Proposed interpretation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[1]... values) {}</code></td><td>Ill-formed. No unexpanded pack.</td><td>A homogeneous pack of the selected type with its own count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[Is]... values) {}</code></td><td>The second ellipsis expands <code>Is</code> into one parameter list.</td><td>Unchanged. No new count.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>


<aside class=issue>
<p>The transition for unnamed <code>Ts...[1]...</code> needs further analysis of C++26 <a href="[STD]dcl.fct#27">[dcl.fct]/27</a> and Clang's parsing, particularly whether the existing form denotes C varargs.</p>
</aside>

### Homogeneous packs of indexed types ### {#indexed-homogeneous}

Scalar indexing fixes the proposed pack's type before count deduction:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I, class... Ts>
auto selected_counter() {
    return [](Ts...[I]... values) {
        return sizeof...(values);
    };
}

auto count_doubles = selected_counter<1, int, double, char>();
</xmp>

<code>count_doubles(0.5, 1.0)</code> returns 2: the index selects <code>double</code> from three types and the call independently determines the count. No index pack remains, unlike <code>Ts...[Is]... values</code>.

### Array parameters ### {#arrays}

In C++26, <code>int... values[Is]</code> expands <code>Is</code> into array bounds, leaving none for an enclosing fold. For example, positive extents 1, 2, and 3 adjust to three <code>int*</code> parameters. Zero extents are independently invalid. <a href="[STD]dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="[STD]dcl.array#1">[dcl.array]/1</a>

For unnamed packs, though, C++26 <a href="[STD]dcl.name#1">[dcl.name]/1</a> has no array-suffix production for abstract (unnamed) pack declarators. P2662R3 removed it with pack indexing. [[P2662R3]]


The proposal retains this grammar. With approved reclamation, an array alias could supply an unnamed homogeneous parameter type with ordinary adjustment and an extent independent of its count. The parenthesized unnamed pack declarators tracked by <a href="https://cplusplus.github.io/CWG/issues/1488.html">CWG1488</a> remain out of scope because the alias needs no new declarator form.


### Empty packs ### {#empty-and-return}

An empty outer <code>Is</code> expansion need not instantiate indexed selection. A fixed index must still designate an element whenever its type is required, including homogeneous lambda declarations with zero-argument calls. <a href="[STD]temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a> <a href="[STD]dcl.type.pack.index#2">[dcl.type.pack.index]/2</a>

## Alternatives ## {#alternatives}

R2 discusses alternative spellings and declaration-site lengths. [[P1219R2]] We retain its rejection of empty template heads and a bare <code>template</code> introducer.

### An additional auto marker ### {#additional-auto}

In <code>int auto... values</code>, <code>auto</code> would mark template status rather than act as a placeholder and <code>int</code> is not a concept constraint. We reject this redundant marker and retain symmetry with homogeneous template packs such as <code>std::size_t... values</code>. <a href="[STD]dcl.spec.auto.general">[dcl.spec.auto.general]</a>

### Explicit template markers ### {#marker}

The <code>template &lt;...&gt;</code> marker considered in R2 could identify the count and permit unnamed packs without reclaiming comma-less C varargs. [[P1219R2]] For lambdas the corresponding spelling would be <code>[]&lt;...&gt;(int... values) {}</code>.

Named homogeneous packs already establish template status ([[#abbreviated-syntax]]). [[P1141R2]] <a href="[STD]dcl.fct#23">[dcl.fct]/23</a> We therefore reject a required marker for a compatibility benefit confined to unnamed packs. An unnamed-only marker avoids that burden, but we prefer requiring a name to adding syntax solely for omitting it.

### Explicit lengths ### {#explicit-lengths}

The invented count model covers the present use cases ([[#abbreviated-syntax]]), though declaration-site lengths would let authors name and position the count.

R2's bracketed length spelling conflicts with C++26 pack indexing: <code>Ts...[Len]</code> already selects one type. [[P1219R2]] <a href="[STD]dcl.type.pack.index">[dcl.type.pack.index]</a> A marker cannot resolve this conflict, but other declaration-site length spellings remain possible.

Multiple or non-trailing homogeneous packs would need rules for associating and deducing their counts. We defer these extensions and declaration-site lengths. Restoring abstract array-pack grammar also remains separate.

# Reclaiming the deprecated comma-less spelling # {#compatibility}

Named packs can proceed in C++29 with existing unnamed disambiguation if EWG declines reclamation ([[#proposed-polls]]).

Jan Schultke's [[P3176R1]], <em>The Oxford variadic comma</em>, chose deprecation because no feature then proposed required removal (section 6.1). Our proposal supplies that reason: reclaiming the spelling would allow unnamed homogeneous function parameter packs.

One standard cycle separates C++26 deprecation and proposed C++29 reclamation. Adding a comma preserves C varargs in earlier language modes, allowing migration before C++29. We cannot assess that migration from the 2019 open-source survey in [[P1219R2]], though. It predates deprecation and therefore establishes neither users' deprecation-warning experience nor remaining migration costs in maintained and vendored code.

There is a one-cycle precedent in [[P1161R3]]'s C++20 deprecation of unparenthesized comma subscripts and [[P2128R6]]'s proposed C++23 replacement. P2128R6 also deliberately withheld new multidimensional meaning from C arrays and existing <code>valarray</code> interfaces, however. It therefore cannot establish that immediate reinterpretation of comma-less varargs would be harmless. If one cycle is insufficient, EWG can choose a longer migration period.

## Current rules ## {#current-comma-rules}

A <em>varargs ellipsis</em> denotes C varargs rather than a parameter pack. Both C23 and C++26 permit <code>...</code> without preceding parameters. C++26 also permits omitting the comma between a parameter list and C varargs, but deprecates that spelling. [[N3096]] <a href="[STD]dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="[STD]depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a>

A final ellipsis that could belong to an abstract (unnamed) declarator declares a pack if its type names an unexpanded template parameter pack or contains <code>auto</code>. Otherwise it denotes C varargs. <a href="[STD]dcl.fct#27">[dcl.fct]/27</a>

These C++26 declarations therefore differ:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// declares a variadic function template with a function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T...);

// same as void f(T, ...)
template <class T>
void f(T...);
</xmp>

## Proposed change ## {#proposed-comma-change}

Under reclamation, we would require a comma between a parameter list and C varargs and remove unnamed disambiguation, permitting unnamed homogeneous packs. If only named packs proceed, we would retain unnamed-ellipsis disambiguation instead. In either case, standalone <code>...</code>, comma-separated C varargs, and ordinary pack expansions would keep their meanings.

<figure class=table id=reclamation-compatibility>
<figcaption id=reclamation-compatibility-caption>Independent declarations before and after proposed C++29 reclamation. In the last row, <code>Ts</code> is an existing enclosing template type parameter pack.</figcaption>
<div class=overlarge tabindex=0>
<table class=data aria-labelledby=reclamation-compatibility-caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>C++26 declaration</th><th scope=col>After reclamation</th><th scope=col>Migration preserving C varargs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>void f(int value...)</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The name precedes the ellipsis</td><td><code>void f(int value, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void f(int...)</code></td><td>Homogeneous pack with fixed element type int</td><td><code>void f(int, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template&lt;class T&gt; void f(T...)</code></td><td>Homogeneous pack with element type T</td><td><code>template&lt;class T&gt; void f(T, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void f(Ts......)</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The final C ellipsis lacks a comma</td><td><code>void f(Ts..., ...)</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</figure>

The six-dot declaration <code>template&lt;class... Ts&gt; struct S { static void f(Ts......); };</code> is valid today. A call to <code>S&lt;int&gt;::f(1, 2.0)</code> passes its second argument through C varargs. To preserve this interface, migration inserts a comma between the ellipses, including in named expansions: <code>Ts... values...</code> becomes <code>Ts... values, ...</code>. Removing the last ellipsis would instead change the interface. [[P3176R1]] <a href="[STD]dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a>

The first and last rows of Table 13 would become ill-formed and require diagnostics. The middle rows, however, would remain valid with new meanings and no diagnostic required merely for reclamation. For example, an old <code>f(1, 2.5)</code> call to <code>void f(int...)</code> passed a double through C varargs. Under reclamation, the same call would initialize two int parameters with an ordinary floating-integral conversion. Reclamation would also permit empty calls and yield fixed-count specializations without C ellipsis. Therefore, even successful recompilation would leave a source and potential link compatibility break under [[SD10]].

### Migrating definitions ### {#migrating-definitions}

Legacy definitions using <code>va_start</code> also need migration, because ordinary homogeneous parameters cannot preserve the actual C ellipsis they require. C++26 discards the second and subsequent <code>va_start</code> macro arguments so a surviving parameter name cannot portably trigger diagnostics. Although implementations may reject a builtin invocation in a non-variadic specialization, C-library preconditions do not require diagnostics. Retaining the C-varargs definition therefore requires inserting the comma. <a href="[STD]cstdarg.syn">[cstdarg.syn]</a>

If declarations and definitions are migrated inconsistently, they can validly declare a homogeneous template and a separate C-varargs overload. Calls may then become ambiguous or select the template. If that template has no definition, the omission can surface during instantiation or linking, though compile or link failure depends on the program, available definitions, and implementation. Changing a declaration provides no ABI compatibility with an existing C-varargs definition.

### Function types and overload resolution ### {#function-types-and-overload-resolution}

Function types also need migration. The alias declaration <code>using F = void(int...)</code> would become ill-formed because function type aliases cannot introduce homogeneous function templates. It must become <code>using F = void(int, ...)</code> to retain C varargs. Reclaimed declarations would no longer match C-varargs pointer targets, though fixed-arity targets could select a specialization by deducing its count ([[#addresses]]).

Reclamation would also replace ellipsis conversion sequences with ordinary parameter conversions, potentially changing overload resolution and answers from well-formed metaprogramming that probes callability, takes addresses, or recognizes C-varargs function types. To preserve C-varargs intent, commas must therefore be retained across declarations, definitions, pointer and member-pointer types, traits, and macro-generated declarations. An ordinary expansion such as <code>template&lt;class... Ts&gt; void f(Ts...)</code> would remain unchanged.

Reclamation also needs to be coordinated with [[P3776R1]], <em>More trailing commas</em>, which proposes trailing parameter-list commas except after C ellipsis parameters. That grammar coordination includes the comma between ordinary packs and C varargs.

# Straw polls # {#straw-polls}

## Proposed EWG polls ## {#proposed-polls}

These proposed direction polls for P1219R3 have not been taken.

1. We want named homogeneous function parameter packs for C++29, independently of reclaiming comma-less C varargs for unnamed packs.
2. We want to remove deprecated comma-less C varargs in C++29 and reclaim the spelling for unnamed homogeneous function parameter packs.


## Historical results ## {#historical-polls}

The polls in Tables 14 to 16 concern earlier revisions, before the C++26 changes addressed here. SF, F, N, A, and SA denote strongly favor, favor, neutral, against, and strongly against.

