Within the instantiation of <code>all</code>, the returned expression expands to <code>((true &amp;&amp; true) &amp;&amp; true) &amp;&amp; false</code>, which evaluates to <code>false</code>.

## Dependency ## {#wording-dependency}

Under our proposal, a homogeneous-pack function type depends on its invented count until that count is determined, even when its element type is non-dependent. Until then, <code>sizeof...</code> naming the pack is also value-dependent. Count substitution still follows ordinary template rules and declaring the pack does not make unrelated expressions dependent.

<aside class=issue>
<p>The dependency and substitution-failure rules need further work for fixed element types with an undetermined count, pack expressions, indexing, and count constraints. This includes invalid indexed element types when the count is zero.</p>
</aside>

## Deduction ## {#wording-deduction}

In <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.general#1">[temp.deduct.general]/1</a>, insert before Example 1:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and addition</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>When a function template specialization is referenced, all of the template arguments shall have values. The values can be explicitly specified or, in some cases, be deduced from the use or obtained from default <var class=grammar ignore>template-argument</var>s.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>For a function template that declares a homogeneous function parameter pack, the argument for its count parameter shall be determined. The argument can be explicitly specified or deduced as described in <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.call">[temp.deduct.call]</a> and <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.type">[temp.deduct.type]</a>. After substitution, the pack introduces the number of function parameters specified by that argument, each formed from its parameter pattern. Explicit specification and deduction of equivalent count arguments identify the same specialization under the ordinary template-argument equivalence rules.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

<aside class=issue>
<p>The wording for substitution order and failure handling is incomplete, particularly when template parameter packs precede the count. The intended order is to apply explicit counts before parameter matching and deduce omitted counts before checking constraints that use them.</p>
</aside>

