### Function calls ### {#wording-call-deduction}

In <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a>, make the inline change and insert before Example 2:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and changes</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>For a function parameter pack that occurs at the end of the <var class=grammar ignore>parameter-declaration-list</var>, deduction is performed for each remaining argument of the call, taking the type <em>P</em> of the <var class=grammar ignore>declarator-id</var> of the function parameter pack as the corresponding function template parameter type. Each deduction deduces template arguments for subsequent positions in the template parameter packs<ins> (if any)</ins> expanded by the function parameter pack. When a function parameter pack appears in a non-deduced context (<a href="[STD]temp.deduct.type">[temp.deduct.type]</a>), the type of that pack is never deduced.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>For a trailing homogeneous function parameter pack whose count argument has not been explicitly specified, deduce that argument as the number of call arguments remaining after those associated with the preceding parameters. If an explicitly supplied count differs from that number, template argument deduction fails. Deduction from the parameter pattern is performed independently for each corresponding argument for template parameters whose arguments have not been explicitly specified. Deductions for the same template parameter, including those from preceding function parameters, are combined under the ordinary deduction rules. If no call arguments remain, deduce an omitted count as zero and make no element-type deductions from the pack.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

Retain the existing <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.call">[temp.deduct.call]</a> example unchanged.

### Function types ### {#wording-type-deduction}

In <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.type#10">[temp.deduct.type]/10</a>, make the inline change and insert immediately after the sentence shown below:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and changes</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>Each comparison deduces template arguments for subsequent positions in the template parameter packs<ins> (if any)</ins> expanded by the function parameter pack.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>When a function parameter list containing a trailing homogeneous function parameter pack is matched against a function parameter list of known length, deduce an omitted argument for the count parameter as the number of parameters remaining after matching the preceding parameters. If an explicitly supplied count differs from that number, template argument deduction fails. Match the parameter pattern against each remaining parameter using the applicable deduction rules. If no parameters remain, deduce an omitted count as zero and make no element-type deductions from the pack. This deduction applies when identifying a specialization from a target function type or from an explicit specialization or explicit instantiation declaration. It does not apply call-argument conversions. In the absence of an explicit count and a function type from which to deduce it, the count remains undetermined and no specialization is identified.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

### Partial ordering and constraints ### {#wording-partial-ordering}

This candidate wording specifies [[#overload-ordering]].

Insert after <a href="[STD]temp.func.order#3">[temp.func.order]/3</a>:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and addition</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>To produce the transformed template, for each type, constant, type template, variable template, or concept template parameter (including template parameter packs (<a href="[STD]temp.variadic">[temp.variadic]</a>) thereof) synthesize a unique type, value, class template, variable template, or concept, respectively, and substitute it for each occurrence of that parameter in the function type of the template. The type replacing a placeholder in the type of the value synthesized for a constant template parameter is a unique synthesized type.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>The transformation preserves each homogeneous function parameter pack and applies the usual transformation to all template parameters, including its count parameter. The parameter pattern is not expanded to the count deduced for a candidate specialization.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

In <a href="[STD]temp.deduct.partial#8">[temp.deduct.partial]/8</a>, make the inline change and insert immediately after the sentence shown below:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Context and changes</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-context"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true"> </span><p>Each comparison deduces template arguments for subsequent positions in the template parameter packs<ins> (if any)</ins> expanded by the function parameter pack.</p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins>For partial ordering, the pattern of a homogeneous function parameter pack is its parameter pattern (<a href="[STD]temp.variadic">[temp.variadic]</a>). Deduction against this pattern combines deductions for template parameters under the usual consistency requirements. Comparison of two pack patterns does not determine their count arguments from the arity of a candidate specialization. A parameter transformed from a homogeneous function parameter pack remains a parameter transformed from a function parameter pack for the purposes of this subclause.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

Existing tie-breaks apply without a new preference for homogeneous packs.

## Declarations ## {#wording-correspondence}

Corresponding homogeneous packs have corresponding parameter patterns and invented count parameters in the same template parameter position. Parameter names do not distinguish declarations. An ordinary pack expansion does not correspond to a homogeneous pack solely because a specialization produces the same function type.

## Feature-test macro ## {#wording-feature-test}

We propose the feature-test macro <code>__cpp_homo_fun_packs</code> in <a href="[STD]cpp.predefined">[cpp.predefined]</a> for homogeneous function parameter packs. We prefer its brevity, but if the abbreviations are too terse, <code>__cpp_homo_function_packs</code> is an alternative choice.

Another option is <code>__cpp_homo_parameter_packs</code>, though it does not distinguish function from template parameter packs. The unabbreviated <code>__cpp_homogeneous_function_packs</code> is unnecessarily verbose and we consider it less suitable.

## Compatibility with C++26 ## {#wording-compatibility}

If the separate reclamation proposal is adopted, add the following entry to <a href="[STD]diff.cpp26.dcl">[diff.cpp26.dcl]</a> in Annex C:

<div class="wording-diff diff-single">
<div class="diff-pane">
<p class="diff-label">Addition, conditional on reclamation</p>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins><strong>Affected subclause:</strong> <a href="[STD]dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a></ins></p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins><strong>Change:</strong> An ellipsis denoting C varargs following a parameter-declaration-list requires a preceding comma. An unnamed parameter declaration ending in an ellipsis can declare a homogeneous function parameter pack.</ins></p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins><strong>Rationale:</strong> Permit unnamed homogeneous function parameter packs.</ins></p></div>
<div class="diff-line diff-insert"><span class="diff-marker" aria-hidden="true">+</span><p><ins><strong>Effect on original feature:</strong> Valid C++ 2026 code that omits the comma before an ellipsis denoting C varargs can become ill-formed or declare a function template with a homogeneous function parameter pack. Calls can change meaning because arguments initialize parameters of the declared element type instead of passing through C varargs. The resulting specializations have fixed-arity function types and do not preserve compatibility with existing C-varargs definitions. Inserting the comma preserves the original meaning.</ins></p></div>
</div>
</div>

# Conclusion # {#conclusion}

With homogeneous packs, a programmer would declare the type a variadic interface needs and let ordinary parameter initialization handle each argument. Abseil could then combine count-dependent results with conversions at the call site, while CasADi could preserve overload selection with one declaration per operation for any supported argument count. In both cases, the parameter list would express the intended behavior directly, reducing the helper code and repeated declarations that programmers must write, maintain, and reason about.

Further design and wording work remains, but these benefits are available with named packs independently of reclaiming comma-less C varargs. We therefore ask EWG to <a href="#proposed-polls">support named homogeneous packs for C++29</a> and treat reclamation as a <a href="#compatibility">separate migration decision</a>.

